MITRE ATT@CK Journey

Discovery (TA0007)


What is Discovery?

One-line definition:
Discovery is the process where an attacker gathers information about the compromised system, users, network, and security tools after gaining access.


Think of it like this:

Attacker enters the house (Initial Access).
Now they look around every room.
They check:

Who lives here?
How many computers exist?
Who is Administrator?
Is antivirus running?
What network is connected?

That is Discovery.


Common attacker commands

Windows:

whoami
Shows the current user.

hostname
Shows computer name.

ipconfig
Shows IP configuration.

net user
Lists local users.

net localgroup administrators
Shows administrator accounts.

tasklist
Lists running processes.

systeminfo
Shows OS version, patches, RAM, CPU, etc.

netstat -ano
Shows active network connections.

arp -a
Shows ARP cache.

dir
Lists files.


Linux equivalents

whoami
hostname
ifconfig
ip addr
ps aux
netstat
ss
uname -a
cat /etc/passwd


Why do attackers do Discovery?

Because they want to know:

Which user has admin rights?
Which antivirus is installed?
Which servers exist?
Is there a Domain Controller?
Where are important files?
Which ports are open?
What software is installed?

The more they know, the easier it becomes to move deeper into the environment.


As a SOC Analyst, what should you look for?

🚩 A normal employee suddenly runs:

whoami
systeminfo
net user
tasklist
netstat

all within a short time.

That can indicate an attacker performing reconnaissance on the compromised host.


Useful Windows Event IDs

Some important Event IDs you may investigate during Discovery include:

4688 → Process Creation
4689 → Process Terminated
4104 → PowerShell Script Block Logging (if enabled)
1 (Sysmon) → Process Creation
3 (Sysmon) → Network Connection
11 (Sysmon) → File Created

Remember: 4688 is especially valuable because it records the command that was executed.

SOC Investigation Questions

Whenever you see Discovery activity, ask yourself:

Who executed the command?
Which user account was used?
Which host executed it?
When did it happen?
Was it normal for that user?
Were multiple Discovery commands run in a short time?
Did the activity continue with Privilege Escalation, Lateral Movement, or Credential Access?


